,"forks":1,"watchers":1,"issues":0,"language":"Python","description":"In the fields of computational linguistics and probability, an n-gram is a contiguous sequence of n items from a given sequence of text or speech. The items can be phonemes, syllables, letters, words or base pairs according to the application. The n-grams typically are collected from a text or speech corpus. When the items are words, n-grams may also be called shingles.[1]  An n-gram of size 1 is referred to as a \"unigram\"; size 2 is a \"bigram\" (or, less commonly, a \"digram\"); size 3 is a \"trigram\". Larger sizes are sometimes referred to by the value of n, e.g., \"four-gram\", \"five-gram\", and so on.","topics":[],"score":229867,"global_rank":112877},{"name":"alexandsquirrel/Neural-Networks-and-Formal-Languages","owner":"alexandsquirrel","html_url":"https://github.com/alexandsquirrel/Neural-Networks-and-Formal-Languages","stars":0,"forks":0,"watchers":2,"issues":0,"language":"Python","description":"Probing how neural networks learn to recognize formal languages.","topics":[],"score":229864,"global_rank":112883},{"name":"nissim-panchpor/PageRank-algorithm-using-Eigonfactor-values","owner":"nissim-panchpor","html_url":"https://github.com/nissim-panchpor/PageRank-algorithm-using-Eigonfactor-values","stars":1,"forks":0,"watchers":0,"issues":0,"language":"Python","description":null,"topics":["eigonfactor-values","graph","pagerank-algorithm","python","social-network"],"score":229863,"global_rank":112884},{"name":"Fiona1121/portfolio-web","owner":"Fiona1121","html_url":"https://github.com/Fiona1121/portfolio-web","stars":0,"forks":0,"watchers":1,"issues":0,"language":"Python","description":null,"topics":[],"score":229846,"global_rank":112906},{"name":"charlottemcclintock/climateAdaptation","owner":"charlottemcclintock","html_url":"https://github.com/charlottemcclintock/climateAdaptation","stars":0,"forks":0,"watchers":1,"issues":0,"language":"Python","description":null,"topics":[],"score":229846